AlexO <alexolog@gmail.com> wrote:
> On Sep 8, 4:03�am, Patric Mueller wrote:
>>
>> Nobody needs the blessing of the DevTeam. There is nothing and nobody
>> that could prevent someone from releasing a "NetHack 4.0.0" like Derek
>> once proposed. But nobody does - so it looks like nobody cares enough
>> to do it.
>
> You are correct in some of your assertions.
> However, the problem (as I see it) is not that "nobody cares" but that
> several people care and the effort is split among several variants
> which are individually too small to gain traction.
>
> IMHO, it would be better if the "less radical" variants merged into a
> single one.

I don't think that it would make a big difference if we bundled our
programming efforts into a single variant.

If there is more than one developer you have to manage them and if
there are different opinions you have to find a consensus. This is
much easier with just one developer.

For a variant to gain traction it just has to become popular not to be
programmed by a group of people.


I think none of us really wants or strives to develop the $NEXT_VERSION
of NetHack. That's after all the DevTeam's job.

At the end of the day the players have only so much time and they
decide what they want to play. And you never know what will happen to
your variant. Maybe they grow and become as popular as those following
two variants?

http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.hack/browse_frm/thread/dea55ac4d1647157
http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.hack/browse_frm/thread/8e0f18b505f53376

> I do not see any deep philosophical differences among Spork, Un and
> Brass.

Yes, there aren't any deep philosophical differences in the sense that
a change in one of the variants couldn't find its way into another
one.

There are already a few changes from SporkHack incorporated into
UnNetHack (mostly those things for what I couldn't think about a
better solution :) and the level compiler changes that Pasi Kallinen
is currently programming for SporkHack also get included rather
quickly.

But each of the three variants has a different focus of what it
intends to be and how it want to get there. Of course there are things
or issues that overlap but more or less the best is if every variant
tries to find its own solution to the problems it is facing.

Even if the solution is just "copy feature X from variant Y".

> Do you think it possible for you, Derek, Youkan (and possibly others
> that I missed) to reach an understanding?

I don't think we would work better together in a team than we do
alone.

We all already started our own variants and because of that have
probably quite strong opinions about the changes already made and the
future changes to the source code. If we worked together we would have
to discuss 

I also don't see the advantage of having just one actively developed
variant. You lose the ability to test different ideas and mix and
match that back into another variant. For example I am not convinced
that percentual resistances as in SporkHack are an improvement or even
fit into a NetHack-like game. But I can play SporkHack and make up my
mind about it and not just discuss it theoretically. That's a big
plus.[1]

Don't think of development of the game as a linear process where there
is only one direction ("forward" or "backward") one can go.

Think of it as a multidimensional sphere where each variant pushes
into a different direction and with each unique change they drift a
bit apart and with each common change the come a bit closer to each
other.

That's not a bad thing. Look how the Angband community works.

> And even if the dev team decides to end their hiatus and releases the
> elusive "next version", their changes could be back-ported into the
> variant.

As far as we know there have been a lot of internal changes to
$NEXT_VERSION's code but all useful improvements and changes would of
course be copied and ported. But what's "useful" that lies in the eye
of the developer. :)

Bye
Patric

[1] Another example is the scoring mechanism of NetHack. It's
generally agreed upon that scoring is completely broken in NetHack.
Arbitrary high scores are possible for every player. But years of
discussions yield almost nothing besides talk. UnNetHack features a
scoring mechanism where the maximum possible score is bounded but the
player can still read his progress from the score. I've come to the
conclusion that the latter is all the players care about.

